# shellcheck configuration for mux. `sh test/run` lints the package with this,
# skipping silently when shellcheck is not installed.
#
# The point of linting here is NOT a clean score. It is the class of defect a
# parse check cannot see: `dash -n` is happy with `for s in $(tmux
# list-sessions)`, which word-splits a session name containing a space and
# which shipped in four separate places before shellcheck was pointed at it.
#
# So the bug-capable codes stay ON, and only the ones that are systematically
# wrong ABOUT THIS CODEBASE are disabled -- each with the reason, so a future
# reader can tell a considered exception from an inherited one. Anything
# genuinely intentional but rare is disabled INLINE at the site instead, so it
# is visible where it matters and a second accidental instance still fails.

# Every library is sourced through a runtime-resolved path ($_lib/<name>.sh),
# because mux self-locates rather than carrying an install prefix. shellcheck
# cannot follow that and says so once per source site.
disable=SC1090,SC1091

# tmux format strings are single-quoted ON PURPOSE: '#{pane_id}' is tmux's
# syntax, not the shell's, and expanding it would be the bug.
disable=SC2016

# `_a= _b=` initialises several variables empty on one line. shellcheck reads
# the space as a failed assignment; it is not.
disable=SC1007

# Variables shared ACROSS the source boundary shellcheck cannot follow: the
# MUX_CFG_* set written by the context fallback stub and read by the front end,
# and positional placeholders in `read -r a b c d` where only some fields are
# wanted. Both look unused from inside one file.
disable=SC2034

# ANSI escapes built as "$_e[1;32m", which shellcheck parses as an array
# subscript. The quoting is correct as written and the alternative is worse.
disable=SC1087

# `[ -e X ] && ok "..." || bad "..."` in the check scripts. The C-runs-when-A-
# succeeds trap needs B to be able to fail; ok() is a printf and cannot.
disable=SC2015

# String values that happen to spell a command: `derive=hash`, `SRC=command`.
disable=SC2209

# A literal tilde in a `case` PATTERN (matching the character, not a home
# directory) and the tilde expansion mux performs itself rather than leaving to
# the shell -- see mux_expand_tilde.
disable=SC2088

# Reading a temp file after writing it in a SEPARATE statement. shellcheck
# warns as if they shared a pipeline; they are sequential passes.
disable=SC2094

# `ls` where the alternative is a find(1) that mux deliberately avoids -- the
# sandbox that runs these cannot traverse some config dirs that globs can read.
disable=SC2012
